  "solution": {
    "text": "<math><mo>(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>)</mo></math> Ionic character <br /><br /><math> <mrow>  <mo>&#x0025;</mo><mtext>&#x2004;</mtext><mi>i</mi><mi>o</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>c</mi><mtext>&#x2004;</mtext><mi>c</mi><mi>h</mi><mi>a</mi><mi>r</mi><mi>a</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>e</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>16</mn><mo stretchy='false'>(</mo><msub>   <mi>x</mi>   <mi>A</mi>  </msub>  <mo>&#x2212;</mo><msub>   <mi>x</mi>   <mi>B</mi>  </msub>  <mo stretchy='false'>)</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>3.5</mn><msup>   <mrow>    <mo stretchy='false'>(</mo><msub>     <mi>x</mi>     <mi>A</mi>    </msub>    <mo>&#x2212;</mo><msub>     <mi>x</mi>     <mi>B</mi>    </msub>    <mo stretchy='false'>)</mo></mrow>   <mn>2</mn>  </msup>  </mrow></math><br><br>where, <math> <mrow>  <msub>   <mi>x</mi>   <mi>A</mi>  </msub>  <mo>&#x2212;</mo><msub>   <mi>x</mi>   <mi>B</mi>  </msub>  <mo>=</mo></mrow></math>Difference in electro-negativity of bonded atoms.<math> <mrow>  <mn>16</mn><mo>&#x00D7;</mo><mo stretchy='false'>(</mo><mn>3.0</mn><mo>&#x2212;</mo><mn>0.7</mn><mo stretchy='false'>)</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>3.5</mn><mo>&#x00D7;</mo><msup>   <mrow>    <mo stretchy='false'>(</mo><mn>3.0</mn><mo>&#x2212;</mo><mn>0.7</mn><mo stretchy='false'>)</mo></mrow>   <mn>2</mn>  </msup>  <mo>=</mo><mn>55.32</mn><mo>&#x0025;</mo></mrow></math><br> <br><math><mo>(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>)</mo></math> Geometry for both the species is tetrahedral, as in both 'O' is <math> <mrow>  <mi>s</mi><msup>   <mi>p</mi>   <mn>3</mn>  </msup>  </mrow></math> hybridised<br /><br /><math><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></mfenced><mi>&nbsp;</mi><mn>113</mn><mo>=</mo><mfenced close=\"]\" open=\"[\" separators=\"|\"><mrow><mi>R</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfenced><mi>&nbsp;</mi><mn>5</mn><msup><mrow><mi>f</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>14</mn></mrow></msup><mn>6</mn><msup><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow></msup><mn>7</mn><msup><mrow><mi>s</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mn>7</mn><msup><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo></math> Due to inert pair effect <math> <mrow>  <mn>7</mn><msup>   <mi>s</mi>   <mn>2</mn>  </msup>  </mrow></math>electron do not take part in bond formation, so, most stable oxidation state is <math><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo></math><br /><br /><math><mo>(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>)</mo></math> Successive ionization energies are higher for every element. <math><msup><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></math> ionization of K happens from <math><mn>3</mn><msup><mrow><mi>s</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mn>3</mn><msup><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup></math> configuration which is an inert gas configuration  and for <math><mi>C</mi><mi>a</mi></math> it happens from <math><mn>4</mn><msup><mrow><mi>s</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math>. So, K has higher second ionisation energy than Ca."
